Proof that a battery which never trips writes exactly the bytes it wrote
before the breaker existed. Measured against the ACTUAL pre-fix tree
(git archive 08c2d7bd1, the tranche base), not against an argument.

  $ git archive 08c2d7bd1 | tar -x -C <scratch>/prefix
  $ (cd <scratch>/prefix && PYTHONPATH=.../src:.../mini python bytes_probe.py)
  $ python bytes_probe.py          # this tree

bytes_probe.py runs run_production_contract_doctor over the committed
_manifest() fixture with an all-admitted executor and dumps
model_dump(mode="json", by_alias=True, exclude_none=True) sorted and
separator-normalised.

  pre-fix  : 39510 bytes
  post-fix : 39510 bytes
  cmp      : BYTE-IDENTICAL

The new optional `circuit_breaker` field is therefore invisible to every
report the breaker does not fire on. It is not "probably invisible": the two
files compare equal byte for byte.

SCOPE, corrected after an adversarial re-run found the broader claim false.
This proves the ALL-ADMITTED case. It does NOT prove that every battery which
fails to trip writes identical bytes: a block with one to nineteen transport
failures never trips (it needs twenty) and still qualifies, but its
`cases[].failure_code` changed from ENDPOINT_ERROR to the new typed codes,
and that value reaches the BUNDLE digest. It does not reach the SUBJECT
digest -- `ReusableQualificationPairV1.pair_payload()` excludes `cases` --
so no home owes a re-battery. That is the honest boundary: the cache key
cannot move; the bundle bytes can, and do, exactly where the legibility fix
is doing its job.
